WEBVTT

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:02.590
Detection engineers rarely work alone.

00:00:02.590 --> 00:00:06.320
Their work sits between telemetry owners, security operations, threat intelligence,

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:10.140
threat hunting, incident response, purple teaming, architecture, DevSecOps,

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:11.355
and business risk owners.

00:00:11.355 --> 00:00:15.730
SOC analysts provide essential feedback because they see how alerts behave during real

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:16.290
investigations.

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:19.890
They can explain which fields are helpful, which alerts create repetitive noise,

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:21.720
and which response paths are unclear.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:25.130
Threat hunters and incident responders contribute different perspectives.

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:28.280
Hunters may identify behaviors that deserve durable detection,

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:32.260
while responders may identify visibility gaps discovered during an investigation.

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:35.960
Threat intelligence helps detection teams prioritize behaviors that matter to the

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:36.795
organization.

00:00:36.795 --> 00:00:41.280
Purple team exercises and controlled validation help teams test whether detections behave

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.250
as intended under realistic conditions.

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:47.334
This collaboration is what makes detection engineering operational instead of theoretical.

00:00:47.334 --> 00:00:50.880
Detections should support the people who use them, fit the environment where they run,

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:53.695
and improve as teams learn from day-to-day security work.

00:00:53.695 --> 00:00:56.890
Collaboration also prevents blind spots caused by assumptions.

00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:59.225
A detection engineer may understand query logic,

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:03.745
while a platform owner understands logging behavior, an analyst understands triage pain,

00:01:03.745 --> 00:01:05.970
and a responder understands incident impact.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:07.620
When those perspectives come together,

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:10.475
the result is usually better than a rule built in isolation.

00:01:10.475 --> 00:01:14.995
The detection is more likely to have the right data, useful context, realistic thresholds,

00:01:14.995 --> 00:01:16.040
and clear next steps.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:18.275
Regular feedback channels make this sustainable.

00:01:18.275 --> 00:01:22.040
Review meetings, detection tickets, analyst comments, post-incident actions,

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:25.640
and validation results can all become inputs to the detection backlog.
